@yuwata, I would appreciate if we could isolate the switching root / device activation fixes from the code that modifies the symlink handling. The former is required to fix actual bugs we are seeing. The latter, while highly worthwhile, is not known to cause any real-world issues.

You are probably aware that the issues we're working on here are outside the realm of CI. Only testing on a variety of diversely configured real systems by real people reveals possible issues. Most of these users don't test with upstream systemd, and won't be easily persuaded to do so.

My patches from #23215 have been verified by users on SUSE releases of systemd, based on systemd 249 and 246, respectively. I'll provide backports of  b5f9f2b7 to these users for testing, but I will not include the rest of your changes because they are too intrusive for us. I hope you can understand that.

comments:
@hreinecke: I haven't forgotten your suggestion. But it  isn't necessary as we're able to determine from the `honor_device_enumeration` flag that we are switching root. When we know we're switching root, we know that `systemd-udev-trigger.service` will run, and that we shouldn't activate any new devices before udev has re-run the rules for them. Therefore sending some sort of message from `systemd-udev-trigger.service` would be redundant. 

Moreover, extending `systemd-udev-trigger.service` would mean that we have to change or reset systemd-internal device states upon an external message, which might be error-prone, and come too late in some situations.

It's true that my current proposed solution is limited to the system boot phase. It doesn't cover a situation where the user changes udev rules (such that the `SYSTEMD_READY` property changes) and manually restarts `systemd-udev-trigger.service` in a fully booted system. But I'm wondering if that's a healthy procedure we want to support in the first place.
